//==
// # The quad grid within the chordal bound (TKT-415, TKT-435)
//
// QuadGrid sizes its rows and columns by the boundary's segment counts alone, so a four-sided
// face curved only inside (the bump solid's B-spline top, whose four edges are lines) became
// one flat quad through its corners: no boundary segment sees the bump. A grid is now kept
// only when every triangle a renderer may draw of it, both diagonal splits of every quad (the
// version 1 contract's rule and the gate's), faces the face normal and lies within
// SurfaceChordal of the surface at the samples refinement tests (DecideGridChordal,
// element-shape.library.plato). Otherwise no grid, and the face gets the refined triangulation,
// which samples the interior. The bound is widened by the model's length tolerance, so a
// cylinder's grid, whose cells deviate by exactly the sagitta its boundary circles meet, stays a
// grid. F1's first rule measured a quad by the nearer triangle on the diagonal from corner 0,
// which keeps a quad twisted along its other diagonal (Law_V1Types_TwistedQuadNeedsBothSplits).
//
// Sections, in order:
//  - Keeping a grid

library FaceMeshingGridChordal
{
    //==
    // # Keeping a grid

    // The grid, unless some element of it that does not collapse at a pole has a rendering
    // triangle that folds when lifted or strays from the surface by more than SurfaceChordal;
    // then no grid, and the face gets the refined triangulation, which splits folds and samples
    // the interior. Decided element by element, stopping at the first refused.
    GridUnlessFoldedOrCoarse(m: BrepModel, f: BrepFaceIndex, r: UvRegion, g: UvTriangulation, c: FaceMeshingCriteria): QuadGridOutcome
        => m.DecideGridChordal(f, r, g, c).GridOutcomeOf;
}
